{"title":"2000 AMC 12 Problems/Problem 21","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><dl><dd><i>The following problem is from the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems\" title=\"2000 AMC 12 Problems\">2000 AMC 12 #21</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_10_Problems\" title=\"2000 AMC 10 Problems\">2000 AMC 10 #19</a>, so those problems redirect to this page.</i></dd></dl>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solutions\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solutions</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Video_Solution)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Video Solution)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(process_of_elimination)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (process of elimination)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_5\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Solution_6\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 6</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Power_Solve\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Power Solve</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Through a point on the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Hypotenuse\" title=\"Hypotenuse\">hypotenuse</a> of a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Right_triangle\" title=\"Right triangle\">right triangle</a>, lines are drawn <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Parallel\" title=\"Parallel\">parallel</a> to the legs of the triangle so that the triangle is divided into a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Square\" title=\"Square\">square</a> and two smaller right triangles. The area of one of the two small right triangles is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> times the area of the square. The <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Ratio\" title=\"Ratio\">ratio</a> of the area of the other small right triangle to the area of the square is \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/c/6bc10c12d8a0ca7aa408a9492883275c01c88b38.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf {(A)}\\ \\frac{1}{2m+1} \\qquad \\textbf {(B)}\\ m \\qquad \\textbf {(C)}\\ 1-m \\qquad \\textbf {(D)}\\ \\frac{1}{4m} \\qquad \\textbf {(E)}\\ \\frac{1}{8m^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -14px\" width=\"491\" height=\"38\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solutions\">Solutions</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solutions\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/e/afe29f5d468b8c91dd67e38cbbc66bd25501eaf9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(36); draw((0,0)--(6,0)--(0,3)--cycle); draw((0,0)--(2,0)--(2,2)--(0,2)--cycle); label(&quot;$1$&quot;,(1,2),S); label(&quot;$1$&quot;,(2,1),W); label(&quot;$2m$&quot;,(4,0),S); label(&quot;$x$&quot;,(0,2.5),W); [/asy]\" width=\"382\" height=\"205\" /></center>\n<p>WLOG, let a side of the square be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Simple angle chasing shows that the two right triangles are <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Similar_triangles\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Similar triangles\">similar</a>. Thus the ratio of the sides of the triangles are the same. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/3/0731d9ff393cebd13c821427f5698642765a7e6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A = \\frac{1}{2}bh = \\frac{h}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"106\" height=\"37\" />, the base of the triangle with area <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/3/c93d829d38f1f73cd97c8fd926e636e968d0c158.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2m$\" width=\"24\" height=\"12\" />. Therefore <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/a/3daca8c3ec4c2728fddc16dfac046782136ee9e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2m}{1} = \\frac{1}{x}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"66\" height=\"37\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> is the height of the other triangle. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/4/4d4864c364cf451134f38278375ad5c08ea34136.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = \\frac{1}{2m}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"62\" height=\"37\" />, and the area of that triangle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/9/5892558dc7127bd9c231a182f68bd41639eed54d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2m} = \\frac{1}{4m}\\ \\text{\\boxed{D}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"163\" height=\"37\" />.\n</p>\n<h3><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Video_Solution.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Video_Solution)\">Solution 2 (Video Solution)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Video Solution)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/HTHveknJFpk\">https://youtu.be/HTHveknJFpk</a>\n</p><p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=TUQsHeJ6RSA&amp;feature=youtu.be\">https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=TUQsHeJ6RSA&amp;feature=youtu.be</a>\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/d/1fd5c48ee1cfa3bcf3bd117fe4798e0440cbb6c5.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(36); draw((0,0)--(6,0)--(0,3)--cycle); draw((0,0)--(2,0)--(2,2)--(0,2)--cycle); label(&quot;$b$&quot;,(2.5,0),S); label(&quot;$a$&quot;,(0,1.5),W); label(&quot;$c$&quot;,(2.5,1),W); label(&quot;$A$&quot;,(0.5,2.5),W); label(&quot;$B$&quot;,(3.5,0.75),W); label(&quot;$C$&quot;,(1,1),W); [/asy]\" width=\"382\" height=\"205\" /></center>\n<p>From the diagram from the previous solution, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" /> as the legs and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> as the side length of the square. WLOG, let the area of triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />\nbe <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> times the area of square <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Since triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> is similar to the large triangle, it has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/7/ab768ad97c18b1ceb8dd0e315623247f7e199e92.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$h_A = a(\\frac{c}{b}) = \\frac{ac}{b}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"129\" height=\"34\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/0/80055446f1dc3fcd55a3ce27463cb11eefb96b58.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_A = c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"51\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/a/5caa9226761852a91d79f81f0de0ea044cd7f2ee.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[[A] = \\frac{bh}{2} = \\frac{ac^2}{2b} = m[C] = mc^2\\]\" width=\"240\" height=\"39\" />\nThus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/5/7c5536dd37c7706e269f9e3aadcb55de05a0c6cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{a}{2b} = m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"60\" height=\"33\" />\n</p><p>Now since triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> is similar to the large triangle, it has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/3/953c4005cb2c2ab4e0f563e595c611c3a01df6cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$h_B = c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"54\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/6/a96c2dc5b355df74e2647c01d26e2e6bb6914003.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_B = b\\frac{c}{a} = \\frac{bc}{a}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"111\" height=\"39\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/b/c6b5a58f0fd075d73ce1504285a534f546542588.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[[B] = \\frac{bh}{2} = \\frac{bc^2}{2a} = nc^2 = n[C]\\]\" width=\"228\" height=\"39\" /> \n</p><p>Thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/5/ad55ea2f2e046c2862d6402227e314a66ee65a29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = \\frac{b}{2a} = \\frac{1}{4(\\frac{a}{2b})} = \\frac{1}{4m}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"181\" height=\"46\" />. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/e/0de96fd32b8c8de6245ca62485df68d32d6e5df8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{\\boxed{D}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"27\" height=\"24\" />.\n</p><p>~ Nafer\n</p>\n<h3><span id=\"Solution_4_.28process_of_elimination.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(process_of_elimination)\">Solution 4 (process of elimination)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (process of elimination)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Simply testing specific triangles is sufficient.\n</p><p>A triangle with legs of 1 and 2 gives a square of area <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/a/f3ada4263c63a9aa0ee9b81bb6933ff609f1731c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S=\\frac{2}{3}\\times\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{4}{9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"124\" height=\"38\" />. The larger sub-triangle has area <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/e/74e8e28c019dfaa3a75e6b5e32c8444c75569511.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T_1=\\frac{\\frac{2}{3}\\times\\frac{4}{3}}{2}=\\frac{4}{9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"129\" height=\"42\" />, and the smaller triangle has area <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/b/2bbab8f67d692f870b5092c02f051359f1e129fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T_2=\\frac{\\frac{2}{3}\\times\\frac{1}{3}}{2}=\\frac{1}{9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"129\" height=\"42\" />. Computing ratios you get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/e/60ec151ed6775a8e82efa1d846004040f57794f5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{T_1}{S}=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"54\" height=\"37\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/c/8dc8448318cb365335845ca59bb32ec7f3dfdaba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{T_2}{S}=\\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"58\" height=\"38\" />. Plugging <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/b/a4b5800f5ad040d308609190ef18a9e0ca08a9b0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" /> in shows that the only possible answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/e/0de96fd32b8c8de6245ca62485df68d32d6e5df8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{\\boxed{D}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"27\" height=\"24\" />\n</p><p>~ Snacc\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5\">Solution 5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/8/0e8102e9cd9a9192faacbc07ff9ae3ec30aa634b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(36); draw((0,0)--(6,0)--(0,3)--cycle); draw((0,0)--(2,0)--(2,2)--(0,2)--cycle); draw((0,0)--(6,0)--(6,3)--(0,3)--cycle); draw((0,0)--(6,0)--(6,2)--(0,2)--cycle); draw((0,0)--(2,0)--(2,3)--(0,3)--cycle); label(&quot;$1$&quot;,(1,2),S); label(&quot;$1$&quot;,(2,1),W); label(&quot;$2m$&quot;,(4,0),S); label(&quot;$x$&quot;,(0,2.5),W); label(&quot;$A$&quot;, (1.25,1),W); label(&quot;$B$&quot;, (4, 2.25),N); [/asy]\" width=\"382\" height=\"205\" /></center>\n<p>WLOG, let the length of the square be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> (Like Solution 1). Then the length of the larger triangle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/3/c93d829d38f1f73cd97c8fd926e636e968d0c158.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2m$\" width=\"24\" height=\"12\" />. Let the length of the smaller triangle be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />.\nTherefore, since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/7/1f78f9368d4ac4bebfb371b8a27e702460e7cde9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A = B$\" width=\"52\" height=\"13\" /> (try to prove that yourself), <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/f/cffba612ed699edfdf746321d8fb936236cc8b38.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 = 2mx$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"71\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/7/b571ad0c554d8523d3f4936f41217ce14ee50cf6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 1/2m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"77\" height=\"18\" />\nThe area of the other triangle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/f/f2f1dafc7bb8f1abd3d05adba738b597e6820229.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1/4m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"45\" height=\"20\" />.\nFrom here, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/e/0de96fd32b8c8de6245ca62485df68d32d6e5df8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{\\boxed{D}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"27\" height=\"24\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_6\">Solution 6</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 6\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Because we know that the right triangles are always similar, in a figure\n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/c/7dc2aedc8013ddc687e997f20ab6da071ca74627.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(36); draw((0,0)--(6,0)--(0,3)--cycle); draw((0,0)--(2,0)--(2,2)--(0,2)--cycle); label(&quot;$y$&quot;,(1,2),S); label(&quot;$y$&quot;,(2,1),W); label(&quot;$a$&quot;,(4,0),S); label(&quot;$x$&quot;,(0,2.5),W); [/asy]\" width=\"382\" height=\"198\" /></center>\n<p>, x/y = y/a. Using cross products, we can say that xa= y^2. We are trying to solve for x because m is in terms of a and y; m= a/2y (the ratio of the area of one triangle to the area of the square). So solving for x, x= y^2/a. Then the question is asking us for the answer to x/2y (the ratio of the area of the other triangle to the area of the square). So x/2y is y^2/a2y which is simplified to y/2a- also 1/4m, the answer.\n</p><p>-Smartgrowth\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Power_Solve\">Video Solution by Power Solve</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Power Solve\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=COfyiOabcCU\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=COfyiOabcCU</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"2\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12\" title=\"2000 AMC 12\">2000 AMC 12</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=20